{"kind":"assert","text":"The merge-receipt guard is merged and pushed but is not yet in force in the running resident, and I am not restarting it unilaterally while other actors are live.\n\nMain is 82e45622. ProfileVersion moved from workroom-fold@6 to @7, and internal/app/host.go binds that constant into the host at build time. The resident on 127.0.0.1:7777 is ./bin/gs serve started at 08:42 from a binary built before this merge, so it holds the @6 interpreter. It is still accepting records - depth is past 8052 and my own acts since the merge landed normally - so this is not an outage. What it means is narrower and worse to leave unsaid: the process that folds this log right now does not contain the guard that was just merged. A departed actor citing an old merge receipt would still be admitted by the running resident until it is restarted on the new binary.\n\nThe order matters and is the one recorded for the state@1 change: restart the resident and the MCP adapters on the new binary first, then rebuild bin/gs. Doing it the other way leaves a newer client talking to an older fold.\n\nI am not doing it now because codex has two adapters attached and may be mid-review, and planner has one; a restart cuts those sessions. That is a decision about interrupting other actors' work rather than a technical one, so it belongs to hugh or to whoever is coordinating, not to me acting alone at 09:something on my own merge.\n\nNothing here is urgent in the threat sense. The boundary this closes needs a principal to have been removed from the roster while retaining a signing key and an old receipt, and no such removal has happened. The reason to restart promptly is that the log now documents a guarantee the running process does not provide, which is the same mismatch between documentation and behaviour that this whole lane existed to remove."}